[ti:South Africa to Make HIV Prevention Rings]
[by:VOA Learning English]

[00:00.24]A South African company will make vaginal devices that protect against HIV.
[00:09.20]Medical experts say that should at some point make them less costly and more easily available.
[00:20.35]HIV is a virus that causes AIDS, a serious disease that affects the body's natural defense system, or immune system.
[00:32.96]The Population Council is an international nonprofit that does medical research.
[00:41.94]It announced recently that the South African healthcare company Kiara Health will start making the devices - rings from the medical product silicone - in the next few years.
[01:05.62]The devices release a drug that helps prevent HIV infections.
[01:12.42]They are approved by nearly 12 countries and the World Health Organization, or WHO.
[01:21.30]The nonprofit group owns the rights to the ring, which are now made by a Swedish company.
[01:29.41]About 500,000 rings are currently available to women in Africa at no cost, bought by donors.
[01:39.90]Ben Phillips is a spokesperson at the UN AIDS agency.
[01:45.86]He said the ring gives women the freedom to use it without anyone else's knowledge or agreement.
[01:55.72]For women whose partners won't use a condom or allow them to take oral preventive HIV medicines, this gives them another option, he said.
[02:10.88]A condom is a device worn by a male partner to prevent pregnancy and the spread of sexual diseases.
[02:21.13]HIV remains the leading cause of death among women of reproductive age in Africa. And 60 percent of new infections are in women.
[02:39.90]Information from WHO shows the ring releases the drug dapivirine in small amounts over a month.
[02:47.48]It currently costs 12 to 16 dollars.
[02:52.50]But experts expect the price to drop once it is widely produced in Africa.
[03:00.30]Developers are also working on a version that will last up to three months, which should also lower the yearly cost.
[03:11.44]WHO has suggested the ring be used as an additional tool for women at high risk of HIV.
[03:21.56]Officials in more than 12 African countries, including South Africa, Botswana, Malawi, Uganda and Zimbabwe, have also approved it.
[03:35.97]WHO pointed to two studies in its approval, saying the ring reduced women's chances of getting HIV by about a third.
[03:48.69]While other research has suggested the risk could be dropped by more than 50 percent.
[03:56.62]I'm Gregory Stockel.
